::summary
Authoritative reference for the app-spec surface of surf-parse v0.9: which
blocks and attributes the parser actually honors today, the two parallel
extraction paths (`extract_app` over top-level blocks vs `extract_all_manifests`
over `::app` children), the field-type and constraint grammars, and the JSON
wire shape of structured validation errors from `validate_app_spec`.
::

This document describes implemented behavior, not aspiration. Anything not
listed as honored below is ignored by the parser even if it appears in a
document.

## Two Extraction Paths

surf-parse has two parallel, non-overlapping app extraction paths. They walk
different blocks and produce different intermediate types.

| Path | Entry point | Walks | Produces |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| A (app format) | `extract_app` (src/extract.rs) | Top-level blocks only: `::schema`, `::route`, `::use`, `::app-env`, `::app-deploy` | `ExtractedApp` |
| B (app spec) | `extract_all_manifests` (src/manifest.rs) | Children of `::app` only: `::model`, `::route`, `::auth`, `::binding`, `::page`, plus infra blocks | `AppManifest` |

Path A ignores `::app`, `::model`, `::auth`, and `::deploy`. Path B ignores
everything outside an `::app` container. A `::route` is honored by both paths,
but only when placed at the matching level.

The `From<ExtractedApp>` bridge (src/manifest.rs) converts Path A output into
an `AppManifest`: schemas map to models, the app-level `auth=` marker (when
captured) flows into `app_auth`, but the structured `auth` (`AuthConfig`) is
always `None` and `pages`/`bindings` are always empty. Bridged manifests
therefore never pass `validate_app_manifest` without a separate auth source.

## The ::app Block

Path B starts at a top-level `::app` container. Honored attributes:

| Attribute | Type | Required | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| name | string | yes (defaults to empty) | App identifier |
| binary | string | no | Binary/server name |
| region | string | no | Primary region |
| port | u32 | no | Listen port |
| platform | string | no | Target platform |
| auth | string | no | App-level auth marker (e.g. `auth=password`); carried verbatim into `AppManifest.app_auth` |

`name`, `binary`, `region`, `port`, `platform`, and `auth` are the only
attributes `parse_app` reads. The `auth=` attribute is a free-form app-level
marker and is distinct from the structured `AuthConfig` produced by a child
`::auth` block: the attribute flows into the additive `app_auth` field on
`AppManifest` (omitted from JSON when absent), while `AppManifest.auth`
continues to come exclusively from the child `::auth` block.

## Models (::model, Path B child)

Honored attribute: `name` (string). Each field is one dash line inside the
block body using the colon grammar:

| Grammar element | Form | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Field line | dash, name, colon, type, optional bracket list | `- title: string [required, max=200]` |
| Constraint list | comma-separated inside square brackets | `[primary, auto]` |
| Enum type | parenthesized variants | `- status: enum(todo, done) [default=todo]` |
| Reference type | parenthesized target model | `- owner_id: ref(User) [required]` |

Unknown field types silently coerce to `string` and unrecognized constraint
tokens are silently dropped by the parser; use `validate_app_spec` to surface
them as `unknown-field-type` / `invalid-constraint` errors.

::callout[type=warning]
Every model must carry a field literally named `id`: downstream crud
generators default their id column to `id`, so a model without one cannot be
persisted. `validate_app_spec` rejects such a model with a `missing-id-field`
error at validation time instead of letting generation fail later. A `User`
model lacking `id` is reported as `missing-user-field` (never both codes).
::

## Routes (::route)

Honored attributes: `method` (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE; anything else
falls back to GET) and `path`. Body lines are key-colon-value:

| Body line | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `auth: required` | Auth requirement marker (free-form string) |
| `returns: list(Task)` | Response model; `list(...)` wraps a collection; `none` allowed |
| `body: Task` | Request body model |

A fenced code block inside the route body is captured verbatim as the
`handler` field (used for embedded Rust handlers). Routes are honored both as
`::app` children (Path B, no handler field on `RouteConfig`) and at top level
(Path A, `ExtractedRoute` keeps `handler`).

## Auth (::auth, Path B child)

The honored auth marker is a child `::auth` block. Honored attribute:
`provider` with these values (anything else falls back to `email`):

| Provider value | Variant |
| --- | --- |
| email | Email |
| oauth | OAuth |
| api-key (also api_key, apikey) | ApiKey |
| token | Token |

Body lines: `session:` (string), `roles:` (comma-separated list),
`default_role:` (also accepts `default-role:`).

## Schemas (::schema, Path A top level)

The separate top-level app format uses `::schema` with a `name` attribute and
a paren grammar — note this is NOT the `::model` grammar:

| Grammar element | Form | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Field line | dash, name, parenthesized type, whitespace-separated constraints | `- email (email) required unique` |
| Sized constraint | colon form | `max:200`, `min:1` |
| Default | colon form | `default:todo` |
| Enum type | colon form | `- status (enum:todo,done)` |
| Reference type | colon form | `- owner_id (ref:User)` |

Unlike the `::model` path, `parse_schema_field_type` and
`parse_schema_constraint` return errors for unknown input (the schema parser
then falls back to `string` / drops the constraint when embedding).

## Field Types

Both grammars share the same `ModelFieldType` set. Accepted spellings differ
by path:

| Type | ::model spellings | ::schema spellings |
| --- | --- | --- |
| uuid | uuid | uuid |
| string | string, str, varchar | string, str, varchar |
| int | int, integer, i32, i64 | int, integer, i64 |
| float | float, f32, f64, decimal, numeric | float, f64, double |
| bool | bool, boolean | bool, boolean |
| datetime | datetime, timestamp, timestamptz | datetime, timestamp |
| text | text | text |
| json | json, jsonb | json, jsonb |
| money | money, currency, price | money, cents, price |
| image | image, photo, picture, img | image, img, photo |
| email | email, email_address | email |
| url | url, uri, link, href | url, uri, link |
| enum | enum(a, b) | enum:a,b |
| ref | ref(Model) | ref:Model |

## Constraints

| Constraint | ::model form (bracket list) | ::schema form (whitespace tokens) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| primary | primary | primary |
| auto | auto | auto |
| required | required | required |
| optional | optional | optional |
| unique | unique | unique |
| index | index, indexed | index |
| max | max=200 | max:200 |
| min | min=1 | min:1 |
| default | default=todo | default:todo |

## Deploy Blocks

Two distinct deploy blocks exist, one per path.

`::deploy` (Path B child of `::app`) honored attributes — a deploy entry is
only recorded when `env` is present:

| Attribute | Type |
| --- | --- |
| env | string (required for the entry to be kept) |
| app | string |
| machines | u32 |
| memory | u32 |
| auto_stop | string |
| min_machines | u32 |
| strategy | string |

`::app-deploy` (Path A top level) honored attributes — only the FIRST
`::app-deploy` block in a document is captured:

| Attribute | Type |
| --- | --- |
| region | string |
| scale | u32 |
| domain | string |
| memory | string (e.g. 512mb) |

Both deploy blocks also collect key-colon-value body lines as free-form
properties.

## Other Honored Blocks

| Block | Path | Payload |
| --- | --- | --- |
| ::use | A | Crate deps: `- serde 1 [derive]` lines, or inline attr names |
| ::app-env | A | Env vars: `- NAME * "description"` lines; star marks required |
| ::binding | B | `source`/`target` attrs plus event body lines |
| ::page | B | `route`, `title`, `layout`, `sidebar` (bool) attrs |
| ::build, ::database, ::env, ::health, ::concurrency, ::cicd, ::smoke, ::domains, ::crates, ::deploy_urls, ::volumes | B | Infra manifest children of `::app` |

## Validation Error Wire Shape

`validate_app_spec(doc)` returns the extracted `AppManifest` on success or a
list of structured `AppSpecError` values. JSON serialization:

| Key | Type | Presence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| code | string (kebab-case error code) | always |
| block | string: app, model, route, or auth | always |
| span | object: start_line, end_line, start_offset, end_offset | omitted when unavailable |
| message | string | always |
| hint | string | omitted when absent |

Error codes:

| Code | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| missing-model | No `::model` blocks (or no `::app` block at all) |
| missing-user-model | No model named User |
| missing-user-field | User model lacks `id` or `email` |
| missing-id-field | A non-User model has no `id` field (crud id column defaults to `id`) |
| unknown-model-ref | A `ref(...)` field targets an undefined model |
| unknown-route-model | Route `returns:`/`body:` names an undefined model |
| missing-auth | No `::auth` block |
| unknown-field-type | Model field type not in the table above |
| invalid-constraint | Model constraint token not in the table above |

Example error for an `unknown-model-ref`:

```json
{
  "code": "unknown-model-ref",
  "block": "model",
  "span": {
    "start_line": 20,
    "end_line": 23,
    "start_offset": 342,
    "end_offset": 431
  },
  "message": "field WorkoutSet.exercise_id references unknown model 'Exercise'",
  "hint": "define a ::model named Exercise or fix the reference"
}
```

## Complete Example

The following live `::app` block parses with today's grammar and passes
`validate_app_spec`:

::app[name=example-app port=8080]

:::model[name=User]
- id: uuid [primary, auto]
- email: email [required, unique]
:::

:::model[name=Task]
- id: uuid [primary, auto]
- title: string [required, max=200]
- status: enum(todo, doing, done) [default=todo]
- owner_id: ref(User) [required]
:::

:::auth[provider=email]
session: cookie
roles: admin, member
default_role: member
:::

:::route[method=GET path=/api/tasks]
auth: required
returns: list(Task)
:::

:::route[method=POST path=/api/tasks]
auth: required
returns: Task
body: Task
:::

:::deploy[env=production app=example-app machines=1 memory=512]
:::

::
